---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title = 'Risk Evidence and Risk Profiles';
const description = 'Entry point for wallet risk profiles, fraud triage, AML notes, and evidence-backed finance AI examples.';
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} canonical="https://bionicbanker.tech/risk-evidence/">
  <main class="page-wrap">
    <section class="hero-section compact">
      <p class="eyebrow">Risk evidence</p>
      <h1>Risk Evidence and Risk Profiles</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        Start here when you want a non-technical explanation of a finance or crypto risk event.
        Each profile separates the signal, the evidence, the limits, and the reader-checkable records before it draws a conclusion.
      </p>
      <div class="hero-actions">
        <a class="btn primary" href="/risk-evidence-overview/">Start with the overview</a>
        <a class="btn secondary" href="/wallet-risk/">See a wallet risk example</a>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="panel-card" aria-label="What risk evidence means">
      <p class="card-label">Definition</p>
      <h2>What is risk evidence?</h2>
      <p>
        Risk evidence is the set of records a reader can check before trusting a risk profile:
        transaction activity, sanctions or watchlist context, fraud signals, timestamps, source notes,
        and the limits of what the evidence can prove. It is not a final legal, compliance, or trading decision.
      </p>
      <p>
        On Bionic Banker, a risk profile should help a non-technical reader see what happened,
        why it matters, what remains uncertain, and which source records deserve a second look.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="content-grid three-up" aria-label="Risk profile entry points">
      <article class="panel-card">
        <p class="card-label">1. Wallet risk</p>
        <h2>Transaction patterns in normal language</h2>
        <p>Follow a suspicious wallet pattern from signal to reason to reader-friendly explanation.</p>
        <a href="/wallet-risk/">Open wallet risk →</a>
      </article>
      <article class="panel-card">
        <p class="card-label">2. Fraud triage</p>
        <h2>Case notes before conclusions</h2>
        <p>Review how evidence, limits, and human-readable triage notes fit together.</p>
        <a href="/fraud-alert-triage/">Open fraud triage →</a>
      </article>
      <article class="panel-card">
        <p class="card-label">3. Full overview</p>
        <h2>Risk Profiles hub</h2>
        <p>Use the full overview for linked projects, public proof records, and related AI finance questions.</p>
        <a href="/risk-evidence-overview/">Open overview →</a>
      </article>
    </section>

    <section class="panel-card" aria-label="Risk profile verification checklist">
      <p class="card-label">Reader checklist</p>
      <h2>What to verify before using a risk profile</h2>
      <p>
        A useful profile should make the source trail visible enough for a reader to check it.
        Before relying on one, look for these four items.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Source records:</strong> links, timestamps, or public records behind the claim.</li>
        <li><strong>Evidence limits:</strong> a clear statement of what the records do not prove.</li>
        <li><strong>Decision boundary:</strong> no legal, compliance, trading, tax, or investment instruction.</li>
        <li><strong>Next review step:</strong> what a human analyst, builder, or reader should inspect next.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
